## Running it

Everything goes through `./run.sh`, which pins the JDK the build expects before it
does anything else. A bare `mvn` picks whatever the machine defaults to, and the
failures that causes look like source errors rather than JDK errors.

```sh
{{if .ContainerRequired}}./run.sh env      # bring up the development stack
{{end}}./run.sh run      # start the application on http://localhost:{{.AppPort}}
./run.sh test     # unit tests
{{if .E2E}}./run.sh verify   # unit tests + integration tests, in production mode
{{end}}./run.sh help     # every task
```
{{if .ContainerRequired}}
`./run.sh env` also takes `down` (keeps the data), `logs` and `reset` (throws the
data away).
{{end}}
## What is in here

| Path | |
| --- | --- |
| `run.sh` | The task runner. Project-independent — nothing in it names this project. |
| `run.conf` | Everything the runner needs to know about this project. The only file to edit when adopting a newer `run.sh`. |
| `run.tasks.sh` | This project's own tasks. A `task_<name>` function becomes `./run.sh <name>`. |
| `.githooks/commit-msg` | Conventional Commits, a 100-character subject, no body. |
| `src/main/resources/META-INF/resources/styles.css` | The stylesheet entry point — `@import` statements only. |
{{- if .Database}}
| `src/main/resources/db/migration` | Flyway migrations. Flyway owns the schema; Hibernate only validates against it. |
{{- end}}
{{- if .ContainerRequired}}
| `environment/dev` | The development stack. |
{{- end}}

## The opinions

Each of these is a decision, not a default. They are listed because the time to
disagree with one is now, while it is still one file.

**`vaadin-core`, not `vaadin`.** The commercial components are not on the
classpath, so no build needs a licence check and no pipeline needs a licence
secret. Swap the dependency when you actually use one.

**Warnings fail the build.** `-Werror`, with the categories excluded that fire on
correct Vaadin and Spring code. A warning nobody has to fix is a warning everyone
learns to scroll past.

**The stylesheet is only `@import`s.** The browser caches each imported partial
separately, so a rule written directly in `styles.css` is a rule that cannot be
cached apart from every other rule. `./run.sh styles` exists because editing a
partial leaves `styles.css` untouched and the browser then serves a stale 304.

**Test ids, not selectors.** Views set `setTestId(...)`, and the tests locate
elements by `data-testid`. A CSS path into the component tree breaks when a layout
gains a wrapper; locating by visible text breaks on a rewording.

**Browserless tests live in the view's package.** They read the view's
package-private fields directly, so a test says what it drives instead of
searching for it.
{{- if .Database}}

**Flyway owns the schema, Hibernate validates it.** `ddl-auto=validate` turns a
drifted entity into a start-up failure rather than a silent rewrite of a running
deployment's tables. A schema change is a new `V2__` file; an applied migration is
never edited.

**`open-in-view` is off.** Otherwise the transaction stays open for the whole
request so a lazy association can be walked from the UI, and a view that waits on
something slow is a view holding a database connection while it waits.

**Tests use a real PostgreSQL.** Testcontainers, pinned to the image the dev stack
runs. An in-memory stand-in tests the stand-in.
{{- end}}
{{- if .Auth}}

**Login is OIDC; this application never sees a password.** It is an OAuth2
*client*: it gets the redirect, the token exchange and the OIDC user. The dev
stack imports a realm with a `dev` / `dev` user, and the client secret in
`application.properties` is a development value in a committed file — a deployment
supplies its own through the environment.

**Every view says who may see it.** View-based access control is on, so a view
with no `@PermitAll`, `@RolesAllowed` or `@AnonymousAllowed` is denied to
everyone. That is the right default, and a surprising one the first time.
{{- end}}
{{- if .Coverage}}

**Coverage is gated where the decisions are.** 80% on `*.service` and
`*.ui.presenter`, not on the project. A project-wide percentage moves when a view
gains a layout, and can be met by testing the easy half.
{{- end}}
{{- if .Traceable}}

**Every build carries its commit.** The enforcer plugin rejects a build with no
`-Dbuild.commit`, so no artefact can exist that cannot be traced to a commit.
`./run.sh` supplies it from `git rev-parse HEAD`.

This is the opinion most likely to get in your way: building straight from an IDE
fails until the IDE passes the property too. To drop it, remove the
`require-build-commit` execution from `pom.xml` and `COMMIT_PROPERTY` from
`run.conf`.
{{- end}}

## Git

This is already a repository, with the first commit made and the commit-message
hook wired up. The hook enforces Conventional Commits, a subject of at most 100
characters, and a single line with no body.

A hook cannot travel with a repository, so a fresh clone has to point git at it
again:

```sh
git config core.hooksPath .githooks
```
{{- if .Traceable}}

The first commit is not a courtesy: this build requires the commit SHA it was
built from (see `build.commit` above), so it cannot run at all until a commit
exists.
{{- end}}
